	abstract = {As generative models for protein design become increasingly accessible, scalable and interpretable biosafety screening is critical. We present a homology-independent pipeline that embeds toxin-derived biochemical descriptors into a PCA/UMAP "toxicity map."High-risk regions are annotated through clustering and anomaly detection, providing an interpretable reference space for red teaming AI-generated peptides. In Pilot A, 1,000 scrambled oxytocin variants revealed that naïve perturbations of a benign peptide clustered near toxin-associated regions. There were 34 flagged by anomaly detection and 47 had ≥ 0.2 similarity to toxins. In Pilot B, 1,000 ProtGPT2-generated peptides overlapped extensively with toxin-defined neighborhoods in ToxDL and T3DB embeddings. While most appeared typical, a subset was flagged as outliers, and several exceeded a stricter threshold of0.4 similarity, demonstrating motif-level convergence with known toxins. These findings suggest that both perturbations and unconstrained model outputs occupy concerning regions of biochemical space. The pipeline provides a reproducible biosafety screen that complements structural modeling and supports safe-by-design protein engineering. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
