	affiliations = {Korea Advanced Institute of Science and Technology, Daejeon, South Korea; Max Planck Institute for Security and Privacy, Bochum, Germany; The Ministry of Unification, Seoul, South Korea},
	abstract = {Where reliable economic data are scarce - as is often the case in authoritarian or otherwise data-limited settings - it becomes difficult to assess economic conditions or evaluate policy outcomes. To address this challenge, we test a human-AI framework that couples unsupervised clustering of satellite imagery with expert-guided ranking to create siScore, a grid-level indicator of economic development that requires no ground-truth data. Moving beyond earlier single-year studies to enable continuous monitoring, we assemble an annual panel for North Korea spanning 2018-2023 and demonstrate the framework's capacity for sustained economic tracking. The series reveals (i) persistent urban-rural disparities: Pyongyang remains the most developed, whereas regions farther from the borders experienced deeper COVID-19 setbacks and slower recovery, and (ii) baseline conditions for assessing the regime's new "20 × 10 Regional Development Policy"Engagement with policymakers highlights a critical deployment barrier - temporal consistency - underscoring the need for robust preprocessing and multi-modal validation before the metric can inform practice. Overall, our work provides policymakers with a viable alternative for tracking economic dynamics where official statistics are unreliable. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
