	abstract = {Early detection and accurate classification of colorectal polyps during colonoscopy are critical for reducing colorectal cancer incidence and mortality, yet conventional screening procedures can miss a considerable proportion of polyps. Although recent advances in artificial intelligence (AI) and deep learning have shown promise in assisting endoscopists, many current solutions remain proprietary and hardware-dependent, limiting broader accessibility. In this pilot study, we employed transfer learning with EfficientDet Lite2 to train three separate models on three datasets—a large publicly available dataset (P1: 28,773 images) and two smaller subsets (P2 and P2’, each 130 images). P2 contained representative images from P1, whereas P2’ simulated real-world conditions by photographing P2 images displayed on a monitor using a smartphone camera. We evaluated the P1-, P2-, and P2’-trained models in two ways: (I) a laboratory-based assessment using 5,409 previously unused images from P1, and (II) a real-world scenario where a Galaxy S9 smartphone captured and processed live video displayed on a monitor. Under controlled laboratory conditions, the P2-trained model outperformed the P2’-trained model, yet in the real-world setting, the P2’-trained model showed superior performance. This contrast underscores the importance of training and testing under conditions that closely mirror actual clinical use. By leveraging open-source tools and widely accessible consumer devices, our approach holds promise for expanding the reach of AI-assisted colonoscopy and ultimately improving patient care. © AME Publishing Company.},
