	abstract = {This paper presents ongoing doctoral research developing a decolonial feminist framework for analyzing artificial intelligence (AI) interventions addressing gender-based violence (GBV). As AI applications proliferate through "AI for Good"initiatives, this PhD study moves beyond evaluating technical effectiveness to examine how these interventions operate within structures of power shaped by coloniality. Drawing on decolonial theory and feminist science and technology studies, the research conceptualizes AI as a techno-political modern project and GBV as a manifestation of the colonial/modern gender system. Initial findings from a systematic literature review reveal that existing technological interventions tend to be reactive rather than transformative, often lacking theoretical grounding and reproducing inequalities. The research proposes a framework for examining AI-GBV interventions through four dimensions: epistemic (whose knowledge counts), governance (who controls development), material (resource distribution), and transformative potential (addressing root causes). The methodology to be carried out in the next steps will combine scoping review, comparative case studies, ethnographic observation, and stakeholder interviews across Majority World contexts. This PhD research contributes to both theory and practice by advancing understanding at the intersection of decolonial thought and critical AI studies, developing evaluation criteria, and identifying approaches that center community knowledge in technological responses to gender-based violence. © 2025 Copyright is held by the owner/author(s). Publication rights licensed to ACM.},
