	abstract = {Hate speech on social media creates escalating cybersecurity risks, particularly within dialectal Arabic where detection remains hindered by the scarcity of annotated corpora. This study introduces KuHateCorpus, the first manually annotated Kuwaiti Arabic hate speech dataset developed to advance dialect-aware online safety research and cybersecurity education. The corpus comprises 2,200 sentences collected from social media interactions, informal dialogues, and simulated scenarios that reflect authentic linguistic usage. Each sentence was labeled as abusive or non-abusive through majority voting by native speakers, ensuring both linguistic precision and cultural validity. To assess internal consistency, supervised classification experiments were conducted using TF-IDF text representations with Logistic Regression and Linear Support Vector Machine models. Across 5-fold cross-validation, Linear SVM achieved 96.8% accuracy and an F1-score of 0.978, while Logistic Regression reached 92.8% accuracy and an F1-score of 0.951. These results confirm the dataset's reliability and highlight the effectiveness of culturally balanced data for dialectal NLP tasks. Beyond its empirical contribution, KuHateCorpus supports the pedagogical integration of hate speech detection into cybersecurity education. It offers a controlled environment for studying bias, interpretability, and responsible AI design within an Arabic linguistic context. By linking computational modeling with cultural awareness, the dataset promotes both technical literacy and ethical reflection, laying a foundation for future research in explainable and multi-dialectal NLP across Gulf Arabic communities.  © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
