	abstract = {Semiconductor lithography is a critical process in integrated circuit fabrication, where precise control over pattern transfer is essential for maintaining feature fidelity at nanometer scales. Conventional Optical Proximity Correction (OPC) techniques apply rule-based modifications to the mask layout but become increasingly inadequate for advanced nodes. Moreover, ignoring phase information leads to poor resolution control and an inability to address phase-induced artifacts near critical features. To overcome these challenges, we propose SwinQuViT, a quantum-inspired, physics-informed Swin Vision Transformer architecture for inverse lithography mask optimization. Our framework incorporates Wigner distribution functions to represent spatial-frequency characteristics in quantum phase space, enabling the model to capture both amplitude and phase interactions. By leveraging the global attention capabilities of transformers, SwinQuViT effectively models non-local dependencies and interference patterns critical for sub-wavelength resolution. Using this approach, we achieve an EPE of approximately 0.15, PVBand of 7,627 and L2 Loss around 6034, resulting in 80% reduction in these parameters as compared to the state of the art algorithms like Neural ILT, GANOPC etc. © 2025 SPIE.},
