	affiliations = {Department of Psychosocial Science, University of Bergen, Bergen, Norway; Department of Psychology, NTNU – Norwegian University of Science and Technology, Trondheim, Norway; Department of Surgical, Medical and Molecular Pathology and Critical Care Medicine, University of Pisa, Pisa, Italy; Department of Psychology "Renzo Canestrari", University of Bologna, Cesena, Italy; Department of Social Studies, University of Stavanger, Stavanger, Norway},
	abstract = {This study examines factors influencing the acceptance of AI across five application scenarios: personalized product recommendations, learning platforms, job screening, traffic control, and medical diagnosis aids. The research focuses on trust, acceptability, ethicality, societal benefit, transparency, skill augmentation, perceived risk, and need for regulation. The findings suggest that perceived risk does not always reduce AI's acceptability or trust towards AI. Despite being highly risky, traffic control AI had the highest levels of trust and acceptance, indicating that the perceived benefit of AI usage can outweigh risk concerns. Ethicality and trust consistently emerged as significant predictors of AI acceptance across all the proposed scenarios. Furthermore, the need for regulatory oversight was more pronounced in riskier scenarios. The study reveals significant differences across scenarios, emphasizing that AI acceptance is highly context dependent. The findings suggest that AI regulation and deployment strategies should be tailored to specific contexts to enhance public acceptance. © 2025 The Authors.},
