	abstract = {Parkinson's disease is the fastest growing neurodegenerative disease worldwide currently. According to American Parkinson's Disease Association, more than 10 million people are affected from this disease in different regions globally. This number is expected to increase to around 20 million in the coming decade. Risk management through early prediction can improve these outcomes but the clinical dependency now largely focuses on subjective examination which often delay the treatment and thereby worsens the condition of the patient. These current methods lack both explainability and interpretability, which is very important in neurodegenerative diseases. Our proposed method introduces a comprehensive framework that is created by integrating high performing machine learning algorithm with explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) and large language model (LLM). It targets to produce predictive accuracy and explainability. This entire system is easily understandable through user interface to the clinical users and patients. Our proposed method started with data preprocessing. After that we extracted the features using systematic evaluation of multiple classifiers. The test results showed that the XGBoost method outperformed other models in terms of accuracy, F1-score, precision, recall, and AUC. But only this prediction scores lacked explainability. To remove this limitations of traditional machine learning algorithms we applied SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) to determine the feature-wise contribution of model predictions that how much influence a feature does have in occurring an individual patient's parkinson disease. It is patient specific and transparent. It demonstrates the reason of Parkinson's disease feature wise. Furthermore, we extended this explainability by integrating it with Meta-LLaMa-3 that transforms the SHAP results into natural language clinical explanation through an user interface. It shows the predictive reasons of the Parkinson's disease and confidence score of having the risk of the disease to the physicians and the patient as a outcome.The proposed framework achieves quite a few benefits by connecting the intermissions between machine learning predictions and real world clinical usability. From determining the disease to explaining the reason why it affected the patient, we also converted it to a user friendly natural language output interface for easy understanding. Most of the previous studies were only focused on predictive accuracy, but our framework uniquely vocal biomarkers and translates these patterns into neurologist-oriented natural language explanations to give a perfect output.  © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
