	abstract = {As deep learning models grow in complexity, they suffer from increasing redundant parameters and high computational costs. Quantum computing offers a new computational paradigm; however, fully quantum AI remains impractical due to current quantum hardware constraints. This raises a fundamental problem: Can quantum AI serve as an efficient module within classical AI architectures to enhance performance while reducing computational overhead? In this work, we explore a hybrid quantum-classical AI framework that embeds parameter-efficient quantum computing modules within classical deep learning models. Specifically, we incorporate a quantum bottleneck module into a U-Net architecture, called QB-Net. Experiments and quantitative analysis in real-world datasets for medical and natural image processing demonstrate that QB-Net reduces up to 36.3× parameters in the bottleneck versus its classical counterpart, while achieving performance comparable to classical U-Net in all metrics considered. Our results demonstrate that quantum AI can serve as a plug-and-play enhancement, leveraging quantum advantages to optimize classical architectures without altering their fundamental structure. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2025.},
