	abstract = {The AI that interacts with children every day through smart toys, learning apps, and chatbots should respect children's rights and support their development. While ethical guidelines are on the rise, they are often vague and high-level, leaving developers to figure out how to apply values like fairness, transparency, participation, and privacy without clear and actionable guidance. By taking into account the developmental psychology and the UN Convention on the Rights of the Child, this preliminary work introduces a Symbiotic Child-AI framework that envisions children who learn through positive interactions with safe, reliable, age-appropriate AI systems. At the same time, AI learns from these interactions, adapting to meet children's evolving needs and feedback, ensuring that appropriate safety measures are in place. The proposed framework aims to provide an immediate and concrete approach to help evaluate and align AI with children's rights, by providing practitioners with a set of practical measures that can be applied to operationalise specific AI-children's rights related principles. The Symbiotic Child-AI framework is a starting point for future investigation and research that supports and prioritises children's rights and development. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
