	abstract = {Prefabricated concrete systems are increasingly promoted for sustainable, rapid, and precise construction, yet their ability to ensure seismic resilience under elevated axial loads (n[jls-end-space/]) remains uncertain. This study introduces an innovative prefabricated shear wall system that incorporates mechanical keyway joints and epoxy-bonded interfaces, designed to enhance inter-module connectivity and structural continuity. Various full-scale wall specimens with different configurations (including non-bonded, epoxy-bonded, and reinforced types) were tested under cyclic lateral loading to evaluate the effects of bonding and reinforcement on seismic behavior. The primary objective is to investigate the seismic performance and resilience optimization of this system through a hybrid experimental Artificial Intelligence (AI) framework. Alongside the Grey Wolf Optimizer (GWO‎), the Whale Optimization Algorithm (WOA) was developed to predict and refine resilience-related indicators such as ductility ratio, peak load, and stiffness degradation. The novelty lies in coupling experimental cyclic data with a metaheuristic AI model to establish a predictive and optimization platform for prefabricated precast wall systems. This approach represents a shift from conventional empirical evaluation toward data-driven performance enhancement, advancing resilience-oriented design strategies for next-generation prefabricated concrete structures. Results show that epoxy-bonded joints increased energy dissipation by 28 34 %, while the GWO WOA model achieved high predictive accuracy (Coefficient of Determination (R²) = 0.98 for Fₘₐₓ and R² = 0.96 for μ). Optimized configurations improved ductility by up to 12 % and peak load by 8 10 %, validating the AI model’s capability for enhancing resilience-oriented design. © 2025 Published by Elsevier Ltd on behalf of Institution of Structural Engineers.},
