	affiliations = {Kunming Medical University, School of Rehabilitation, Kunming, 650500, China},
	abstract = {Artificial intelligence (AI), as an emerging scientific paradigm, is pivotal in fostering interdisciplinary research and driving knowledge discovery. To map the knowledge evolution and technological integration of AI in rehabilitation science, this study proposes rehabilitation knowledge discovery (RehabKD), a multi-model collaborative AI framework, and investigates its potential for exploring literature data, mining insights, and constructing a knowledge graph. Our approach encompasses three phases: 1) Data Exploration, involving sentiment analysis of titles to assess emotional dynamics, unsupervised dimensionality reduction of semantic vectors (using manifold learning and variational autoencoders), and exploratory clustering (e.g., K-Means, HDBSCAN) of the semantic space; 2) Data Mining, featuring deep mining of community structures in co-occurrence networks and temporal topic evolution analysis using Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA); and 3) Knowledge Discovery, enabled by our novel large language model - knowledge graph - latent dirichlet allocation and complex network co-occurrence matrix model (LLM-KG-LDACX). These methods were evaluated with metrics grouped by objective: sentiment analysis used polarity/subjectivity indices; semantic clustering used Silhouette Score, Calinski Score, and Davies Score (to compare strategies); complex network community detection used degree distribution and clustering coefficient (to measure keyword neighborhood features); knowledge graph construction used accuracy, completeness, connectivity, and redundancy (to compare strategies); additionally, entity recognition statistics, external validation support, relationship extraction statistics, external co-occurrence support, and external exact match support served for PubMed external validation and method comparison. The results demonstrate that our multi-model framework effectively facilitates rehabilitation knowledge discovery, establishing a 'data-driven to model collaboration to knowledge graph to disciplinary mapping' paradigm. Crucially, without requiring labeled data for training, the LLM-KG-LDACX model extracted significantly more valid entities and relationships from the target dataset than supervised benchmarks (biLSTM-CRF, BERT) and an LLM-only baseline, as confirmed by external PubMed validation. This work provides rehabilitation science with a reusable analytical framework (RehabKD) and a powerful knowledge engine (LLM-KG-LDACX), offers empirical evidence for AI's role as a 'scientific collaborator,' and contributes to optimizing research decision-making. © 2013 IEEE.},
