	abstract = {This study investigates the safety behavior of open-source generative AI models acting as smart home assistants for IoT device control. A total of 140 prompts were constructed across seven categories derived from international IoT security and AI risk frameworks (e.g., ETSI EN 303 645, NIST AI RMF), covering both routine and high-risk scenarios such as emergency overrides, privacy-sensitive requests, and adversarial misuse. Six models - base and instruction-tuned variants of Llama 3.1, Qwen 2.5, and Gemma 7B - were evaluated using a hybrid method combining keyword-based pattern matching with LLM-as-a-judge scoring, leveraging a safety-aligned Mistral model. Instruction-tuned models showed noticeable improvements in categories involving bias, abnormal input, and misuse, but residual vulnerabilities were still observed in authorization and privacy-related prompts. These findings underscore the limitations of current LLMs in context-sensitive reasoning and highlight the need for scenario-specific safeguards. This pilot study offers a reproducible benchmark and methodological framework for safety-aligned LLM evaluation in embedded environments. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
