	pages = {11425 - 11454},
	doi = {10.1007/s10115-025-02560-w},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105020723470?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {MIPT University, Dolgoprudny, Russian Federation; HSE University, Moscow, Russian Federation; IITP RAS, Moscow, Russian Federation},
	abstract = {Widespread deployment of societal-scale machine learning systems necessitates a thorough understanding of the resulting long-term effects these systems have on their environment, including loss of trustworthiness, bias amplification, and violation of AI safety requirements. We introduce a repeated learning process to jointly describe several phenomena attributed to unintended hidden feedback loops, such as error amplification and induced concept drift. A distinctive feature of such a repeated learning setting is that the state of the environment becomes causally dependent on the learner itself over time, thus violating the usual assumptions about the data distribution. We present a novel mathematical model of the repeated learning process, prove the sufficient convergence conditions, and find the limiting set of probability distributions for the system’s positive and negative feedback loop modes of operation. We conduct computational experiments using an exemplary supervised learning problem on two synthetic data sets. The results of the experiments correspond to the theoretical predictions derived from the mathematical model. Our results demonstrate the feasibility of the proposed approach for studying the repeated learning processes in machine learning systems and open a range of opportunities for further research in the area. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer-Verlag London Ltd., part of Springer Nature 2025.},
