	affiliations = {Department of Civil and Water Resources Engineering, National Chiayi University, Chiayi, 600355, Taiwan},
	abstract = {Study region: Taiwan, a subtropical island in East Asia with complex topography and diverse climatic conditions. Study focus: Motivated by the sparse distribution of Class A evaporation pans in Taiwan, this study develops an artificial intelligence-based framework to estimate daily pan evaporation across Taiwan without relying on ground-based meteorological station data. By integrating high-resolution reanalysis inputs with station metadata, the framework enables spatially continuous estimation of evaporation patterns. The performance and interpretability of the proposed framework were systematically evaluated to ensure its robustness for ungauged estimation. New hydrological insights for the region: XGBoost achieved the best overall performance, with MAE = 0.92 mm/day, CC = 0.72, CE = 0.50, KGE = 0.58, and PBIAS = 1.30 %, highlighting its strong potential for estimating pan evaporation in ungauged regions. The framework not only captured dominant meteorological drivers such as radiation, temperature, humidity, and wind speed but also revealed hidden influences like precipitation and seasonal cycles through SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) analysis. Regional case studies confirmed that the model can reproduce both spatial and seasonal variability of evaporation, with higher rates in coastal lowlands and lower rates in mountainous areas. By bridging sparse ground observations with gridded reanalysis inputs, this study provides spatially continuous and interpretable evaporation estimates, offering practical benefits for agricultural water management, reservoir operation, and climate adaptation in Taiwan’s complex hydrological settings. © 2025 The Author(s). Published by Elsevier B.V. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license. http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/},
