	affiliations = {Carnegie Mellon University, Pittsburgh, PA, United States},
	abstract = {Gender inequality in public administration, especially in legal and policy leadership, remains a critical barrier to equity worldwide. This work presents the first globally-scaled, AI-driven framework to systematically analyze and address this challenge. Using supervised machine learning, causal inference, and natural language processing, we construct a robust system that identifies structural barriers to women's representation and links them to measurable policy and equity outcomes. Our framework shows how AI can navigate the complexity, scale, and nuance required to study representation globally. Unlike traditional approaches, it extracts actionable insights from diverse data sources, including unstructured legal texts and policy metrics across more than 190 countries. The findings reveal key predictors of women's underrepresentation, demonstrate the causal effects of representation on national equity outcomes, and underscore the gap between legal discourse and actual patterns of representation. This work offers a scalable framework for applying trustworthy AI in public interest domains, demonstrating how interpretable and data-driven methods can advance equity while enhancing transparency and accountability in governance. © 2025 The Authors.},
