	affiliations = {Department of Humanities, Social Sciences & Management, National Institute of Technology Jamshedpur, Jharkhand, Jamshedpur, India; Department of Management Studies, Galgotias College of Engineering and Technology, Knowledge Park-II, Greater Noida, 201310, India},
	abstract = {Purpose: This study investigates the role of Digital Environmental, Social, and Governance (DESG) frameworks as a catalyst for advancing the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs). It employs a dual-method approach to map the evolution of DESG research, evaluate regional and sectorial disparities, and identify actionable pathways for aligning technological innovation with equitable sustainability outcomes. Design/methodology/approach: A dual-method approach combining Bibliometric analysis and systematic review was applied to 589 peer-reviewed publications (2018–2025) from Scopus and Web of Science. Techniques included co-citation networks, keyword co-occurrence, thematic mapping, and performance analysis to assess growth trends, collaboration dynamics, and intellectual structures. Findings: Post-2020 research surged by 25.85% annually, driven by AI, blockchain, and IoT, with China leading empirical contributions (47 publications, 506 citations). Thematic clusters emphasized digital governance, ESG transparency, and circular economies. Critical gaps included underrepresentation of Africa and South Asia, fragmented global collaborations, and limited empirical validation in healthcare (SDG 3) and agriculture (SDG 2). Declining citations for recent studies highlighted the need for sector-specific applications and scalable models in low-resource settings. Research limitations implications: The analysis excludes non-academic sources and non-English publications, potentially overlooking regional insights. Future studies should prioritize context-specific DESG frameworks, ethical AI governance, and inclusive collaborations to address disparities in digital infrastructure and knowledge dissemination. Originality/value: This research offers the first holistic synthesis of DESG's role in SDG advancement, integrating Bibliometric insights with actionable strategies. It uniquely highlights China's tech-driven sustainability models, Switzerland's ethical policy frameworks, and Africa's empirical gaps, proposing scalable solutions for resilient, stakeholder-centric digital governance. © 2025},
