	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035502566?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {School of Medicine, Nankai University, Tianjin, 300071, China; Henan University of Technology, Henan, Zhengzhou, 450001, China},
	abstract = {Ion channels are critical regulators of neuronal development, excitability, and homeostasis. Dysfunctions in ion channel expression, structure, or regulation—collectively termed channelopathies—have been implicated in a broad spectrum of brain disorders, including epilepsy, autism spectrum disorder, depression, and Alzheimer’s disease. However, the structural complexity, conformational plasticity, and spatiotemporal heterogeneity of ion channels have historically posed significant challenges to mechanistic understanding and therapeutic targeting. Recent breakthroughs in artificial intelligence are reshaping this landscape by introducing powerful tools for structural prediction, functional modeling, disease subtyping, and drug discovery. In this review, we highlight how AI techniques—such as AlphaFold2, deep learning–based electrophysiological simulation, and multimodal data integration—are advancing the study of ion channelopathies. We discuss applications in identifying disease-specific ion channel dysregulation, stratifying brain disorder subtypes using imaging and molecular features, and accelerating the discovery of channel-targeting compounds through virtual screening, structure–activity relationship modeling, and in silico toxicity prediction. Moreover, we explore how AI frameworks are enabling precision neuroscience by integrating single-cell transcriptomics, patient-derived cellular models, and explainable machine learning algorithms to prioritize individualized therapeutic targets. Despite these promising advancements, existing research faces several limitations, including incomplete experimental validation, limited generalizability across disease models, and insufficient integration of real-world clinical data. Additionally, the practical implementation of AI in this domain must overcome challenges such as data quality variability, algorithmic bias, and the lack of model interpretability, which may hinder clinical translation and regulatory acceptance. Addressing these challenges will be crucial to fully realize the potential of AI-driven ion channel research and its application in precision brain medicine. © 2025 SPIE.},
