	abstract = {The impact of Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR) on brand behavior has become a critical area of study in recent years. However, there is a lack of comprehensive understanding of how CSR initiatives influence consumer brand behaviors. To fill the existing gap, this study provides an empirical study of four novel constructs from an employees’ perspective, namely, perceived corporate digital responsibility, digital brand understanding, digital brand love and digital brand citizenship behavior. Especially, we propose a conceptual model based on structural equations modeling with data collected from 256 Google’s employees in Taiwan to do the analysis. The findings proved the role of perceived corporate digital responsibility on enhancing digital brand understanding and digital brand citizenship behavior. Regarding the effect of digital brand understanding on digital brand love, the result suggest that digital brand understanding positively impacts digital brand love. In addition, the result identified how employees develop a “reason” to exhibit voluntary digital brand behaviors through digital brand understanding and digital brand love. The results reveal that perceived corporate digital responsibility is a crucial antecedent of digital understanding and digital brand love, which in turn influencing digital brand citizenship behavior. This has a certain guiding significance for business management planning to some extent. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s)},
