	abstract = {Paraphrase detection is a pivotal task in natural language processing (NLP), with far-reaching implications for applications of artificial intelligence (AI) such as content moderation, machine translation, and misinformation detection. Despite advances in high-resource languages, it remains challenging for low-resource languages like Arabic and Urdu due to their complex syntax, morphology, flexible word order, and scarce annotated datasets. Cross-lingual transferability, particularly between typologically distinct languages (e.g., Arabic [Semitic] and Urdu [Indo-Aryan]), is understudied. To bridge this gap, we introduce the Multi-view Siamese Dual-Contextual Paraphrase Detection Network (MSDCPDN), a semi-supervised implemented AI framework specifically designed for paraphrase detection in low-resource multilingual contexts. The novelty of MSDCPDN lies in two key innovations: (i) a dual-contextual attention mechanism within a Siamese network that simultaneously captures fine-grained lexical-semantic relationships and global syntactic dependencies, overcoming the limitations of traditional methods, and (ii) a teacher–student knowledge distillation strategy, which transfers robust linguistic representations from a high-resource Arabic model to a lightweight student model optimized for Urdu. Designed for AI applications in low-resource settings, experimental results demonstrate that MSDCPDN achieves state-of-the-art accuracy in Arabic paraphrase detection and generalizes effectively to Urdu without target-language training, demonstrating strong interlingual transfer. This work bridges the gap in cross-lingual paraphrase detection through linguistically informed AI, emphasizing scalability for languages with structural disparities. The implemented AI framework and its application of AI techniques offer practical solutions for real-world challenges in misinformation mitigation and automated content analysis, underscoring the viability of resource-efficient, transferable NLP systems. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd},
