	affiliations = {Government College of Engineering, Tirunelveli, India},
	abstract = {Artificial Intelligence (AI) is rapidly emerging as a transformative force in global development, offering new capabilities to accelerate progress across the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs). This chapter explores the multidimensional role of AI within the broader context of digital maturity and ecosystem readiness. It begins by mapping core AI capabilities-such as machine learning, natural language processing, computer vision, and predictive analytics-to specific SDG targets, illustrating their relevance to sectors including health, education, agriculture, energy, and financial inclusion. It uses global case studies to show how AI contributes to better service delivery, risk prediction and targeted development programs. It further explores the negative sides of using AI, for example, algorithmic bias, privacy issues, the effects it has on the environment and the worsening of digital divide. These issues are discussed using the term digital maturity, highlighting how infrastructure, organizational strength and open governance matter. It presents ideas for responsible AI based on ethics, laws that protect rights, inclusive design and projects that help the public. It recommends that governments, development agencies and stakeholders in technology create AI strategies that are easy to understand, open to everyone, adaptable and follow what is important to their communities. It is argued by the chapter that, though AI by itself is not perfect, it can greatly assist in promoting sustainable development, as long as it is directed by equity, accountability and global cooperation. © 2026, IGI Global Scientific Publishing. All rights reserved.},
