	abstract = {Artificial intelligence (AI) is revolutionizing medical literature retrieval by addressing the inefficiencies of traditional keyword-based methods in the era of “New Medical Science”. Confronted with information overload and stagnant knowledge updates, AI technologies-including natural language processing (NLP), knowledge graphs, and federated learning-enable semantic comprehension, dynamic knowledge discovery, and secure cross-institutional collaboration. Transformer-based models (e.g., BERT, GPT-4) enhance precision by interpreting contextual semantics, while recommendation systems personalize literature delivery through user behavior analysis. AI also safeguards integrity via plagiarism detection and blockchain-based data provenance. Challenges persist, such as terminological diversity and overreliance on AI tools, necessitating domain-specific model refinement, explainable AI frameworks, and hybrid pedagogy blending AI assistance with critical thinking training. Ethical risks are mitigated through federated learning with differential privacy and three-tier oversight. Future directions emphasize specialty-specific AI models, immersive Extended Reality (XR) learning environments, and global collaboration via initiatives like the “Digital Silk Road” for standardized data sharing. By integrating technological innovation with interdisciplinary education, AI empowers researchers to navigate evolving medical frontiers, fostering data-knowledge dual-driven talent aligned with precision medicine and smart healthcare paradigms. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
