	abstract = {Cycling is widely recognized as a scalable solution for reducing urban congestion, greenhouse gas emissions, and improving public health. Yet, cycling adoption rates remain low in many cities due to persistent infrastructure gaps, insufficient policy incentives, and fragmented digital strategies. This article advances a novel framework combining artificial intelligence (AI), gamified urban networks, and targeted policy incentives—explicitly anchored in global best practices for road planning and route connectivity—to accelerate urban cycling uptake. The framework highlights how AI-powered dashboards, user segmentation (including age- and condition-based targeting), and reward systems can motivate participation while supporting continuous, data-driven infrastructure planning. Emphasizing the foundational need for safe, interconnected cycling networks, the study advocates for mission-critical investments in physical infrastructure, context-sensitive policy design, and public–private partnerships. By leveraging behavioral insights and smart technology atop robust urban planning, cities can mainstream cycling as a healthy, inclusive, and climate-positive transport mode. The article concludes with actionable recommendations for policymakers, business leaders, and civic groups committed to advancing a cycling revolution through integrated, evidence-based strategies. © 2025 The Author(s).},
