	abstract = {This study explores the rise of AI-driven virtual anchors by integrating Don Norman’s three-level framework of emotional design (visceral, behavioral, reflective) with insights from media studies, digital culture, and AI ethics. Unlike prior research that primarily focuses on technical capabilities, this study emphasizes the emotional, cultural, and interactive dimensions of virtual anchors. Methodologically, it is a conceptual and theoretical study based on literature review and illustrative case analysis (e.g., VTubers such as Kizuna AI and XiaoIce). Data are drawn from secondary sources, including industry databases (e.g., Hololist) and prior scholarly work. Findings highlight how design features, like visual style, voice performance, and interactivity, affect audience engagement and parasocial connections. The study contributes by foregrounding emotional design as a critical but underexplored lens for understanding AI anchors in digital culture. Limitations include reliance on secondary data, Japan-centric examples, and a lack of direct user-centered validation. Future research should empirically test how emotional design influences user trust, engagement, and ethical perception of AI anchors. © 2025, IPMEDIA SDN BHD. All rights reserved.},
