	abstract = {This chapter critically explores the intersections of artificial intelligence (AI) and democratic public deliberation through Michel Foucault's concepts of utopia and heterotopia. It examines how AI technologies simultaneously open new spaces for inclusive, transparent engagement while also reinforcing power asymmetries and exclusion. Drawing on international case studies-from AI-mediated consultations and participatory budgeting to anti-corruption tools-the chapter reveals how AI serves as both an enabler and a disruptor of democratic ideals. Central to this analysis is the evolving role of communicators, called to move beyond organizational mandates and assume a societal logic. As mediators between AI systems and the public, communicators have a critical role in surfacing blind spots, fostering inclusive discourse, and shaping narratives that align technological innovation with democratic values rather than undermine them. By reframing communicators as societal stewards navigating these emerging "other spaces, " the chapter highlights the urgent need for ethically grounded, participatory, and inclusive approaches to AI governance. © 2026 by John Wiley & Sons, Inc. All rights reserved.},
