	affiliations = {Laboratoire QUARTZ (EA7393), Isae-Supméca, 3 rue Fernand Hainaut, Saint Ouen Cedex, 93407, France; LA2MP (LR13ES25), Laboratoire de Mécanique, Tunisia; ISET Sfax, DGET, Route de Mahdia Km 2.5 BP 88 A, Sfax, 3099, Tunisia},
	abstract = {Lightweight sandwich panels are increasingly used in transportation and building for their structural strength and thermal-acoustic insulation. However, optimizing natural-fiber bio-composites remains challenging because traditional trial-and-error approaches struggle to capture their complex, nonlinear thermomechanical behavior. This study introduces a hybrid Taguchi–Artificial Intelligence (AI) framework, to optimize LDPE/Alfa/PU sandwich panels subjected to thermomechanical loading, aiming to minimize peak normal and shear stresses during the compression molding process. Sixteen Taguchi experiments generated predictive models for key mechanical and thermal properties, Young's modulus, tensile strength, elongation at break, melting temperature, glass‑transition temperature, and specific heat capacity. These models were then embedded in a digital twin architecture, which explored 3000 virtual scenarios using Artificial Neural Networks (ANNs) coupled with Differential Evolution algorithms. The optimization revealed that injection temperatures between 185–190 °C, Alfa fiber contents of 15–20 wt%, and PU core thicknesses from 4–10 mm significantly enhance the panel's stiffness while maintaining stresses below critical thresholds. Overall, this integrated Taguchi–AI methodology provides a robust, accurate, and scalable approach for the sustainable manufacturing of high-performance natural fiber-reinforced sandwich panels. © 2025},
