	abstract = {Effective decision-making with AI-enabled technologies hinges on trust and the integration of trust-assurance mechanisms into system design. This paper builds the rationale for adopting a Human-Centered AI (HCAI) approach to embed trust-related values into the design of AI systems, especially in high-stakes domains such as healthcare. We present a study that demonstrates how interaction designers can apply a user-centered methodology to serve as lenses to design Clinician-Centered Explanations for Dementia Risk Prediction. Our study was built from a literature review to examine the State of the Art of User-Centered Approaches to Enhance Existing XAI Models. This was further complemented by a co-design workshop involving two clinicians and one engineer to explore participants' expectations and preferences for visual and textual explanations to aid the interpretation of XAI model outputs. The findings highlight that the perceived effectiveness of AI explanations is strongly influenced by their alignment with clinicians' roles, mental models, and use of domain-specific language. Based on these insights, the outcomes are synthesized to derive practical design implications and strategies for tailoring XAI interfaces to diverse clinical stakeholders. These findings contribute to the field of human-centred XAI by offering actionable guidance for designing transparent and trustworthy AI systems. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
