	abstract = {Thermal runaway (TR) in lithium-ion batteries remains a critical safety challenge for electric vehicles. This review systematically explores TR mechanisms, including triggering factors, signal evolution, and advanced early warning methods. It highlights key limitations of conventional single-signal approaches, such as voltage and temperature monitoring, which lack robustness under dynamic conditions and provide limited lead time. To address these gaps, recent advances in multi-signal fusion frameworks and data-driven models are comprehensively evaluated. The integration of electrical, thermal, gas, and mechanical signals demonstrates improved detection accuracy; however, practical deployment is constrained by sensor cost, packaging complexity, and cybersecurity concerns. Comparative analysis of machine learning and physics-informed approaches reveals a trade-off between prediction accuracy and interpretability. This review identifies major challenges, including insufficient early-warning generalization, a lack of standardized performance benchmarks, and limited scalability in industrial settings. Accordingly, a future roadmap is proposed emphasizing synthetic data expansion, lightweight adaptive models for embedded systems, and cloud-integrated diagnostic platforms. This work bridges theoretical progress and application needs, offering scalable strategies such as edge intelligence and hybrid AI frameworks to enhance real-time battery safety management. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd},
