	abstract = {AI has rapidly transformed societies, economies, and governance, but it has also exposed critical challenges in global AI regulation. This chapter examines the contrasting governance models of the European Union, the United States, and China- each reflecting distinct values and strategic interests. These differences have led to fragmented global AI governance, creating compliance burdens, stifling innovation, and risking human rights. To address this, the chapter proposes a hybrid governance model combining national sovereignty with global interoperability. This model is built on three tiers: universal norms, national and regional frameworks, and sectorspecific adaptations. It balances data, technological, and normative sovereignty, providing a scalable solution for ethical AI governance. The chapter concludes by assessing the model's feasibility, emphasizing the need for international cooperation to ensure responsible, adaptable, and coherent AI regulation worldwide. © 2026, IGI Global Scientific Publishing. All rights reserved.},
