	affiliations = {The Independent Institute of Education, South Africa},
	abstract = {Artificial intelligence (AI) is not only changing the way that people work, live and acquire information, but it also impacts significantly on international relations and global power shifts between states. This is being revealed dynamically through different approaches to the regulation of AI, which in turn also reflects the intention of certain states or regions to stake their claim in what is known today as a "Global AI Race". These events indicate how AI adoption can shift global power, encourage competitiveness and foster innovation. However, this begs the question of whether such a global race is one that has "only one winner" at the expense of all others or one that must prioritise global collaboration, human-centricity and fundamental rights in achieving responsible AI for all where every human being wins. This chapter therefore explores how a therapeutic techno-political approach, supported by principles informed by fundamental rights, can amplify collaboration and human-centric values to empower people in their political communities where technology like AI plays key roles. © 2026, IGI Global Scientific Publishing. All rights reserved.},
