	abstract = {As Artificial Intelligence (AI) systems become increasingly embedded in children's daily lives, shaping learning, play, and access to information, the need to align these technologies with children's rights has become both urgent and complex. International frameworks such as those developed by IEEE, UNICEF, the World Economic Forum, and the 5Rights Foundation, offer guidance grounded in fairness, transparency, and participation, but there is still a systemic shortfall in translating these rights into technical practice. This paper analyzes five leading child-AI ethics frameworks and identifies common limitations, such as the absence of measurable evaluation metrics and a lack of post-deployment monitoring mechanisms. Drawing on this analysis, we propose directions for advancing operational standards through developmentally appropriate metrics, dynamic evaluation tools, and structured co-design methods. The aim of this paper is to move beyond aspirational principles toward practical instruments that support the design of AI systems that are not only safe and inclusive but also rights-respecting by design. We invite interdisciplinary collaboration to co-develop evaluation models that reflect children's lived realities and evolving developmental needs. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
