	abstract = {This study explored how AI affects the sustainability of competitive advantage in the tourism and hospitality sector, with a particular focus on the mediating roles of digital culture and digital skills in the lens of the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM). Data were collected via a structured questionnaire distributed to a purposive sample of 488 managers and supervisors working in five-star hotels, travel agencies, and DMCs across Saudi Arabia. The findings revealed that AI has a significant direct effect on sustainable competitive advantage and also exerts strong positive effects on both digital culture and digital skills. In turn, both of these internal enablers significantly contribute to sustaining a competitive advantage. Mediation analysis further showed that both digital culture and digital skills partially mediate the relationship between AI and sustainable competitiveness. The study addresses a notable gap in tourism research by providing localized evidence from a market undergoing rapid transformation under Vision 2030, and, taken together, extends TAM to an organizational lens by demonstrating AI’s role in shaping culture and skills that underpin a durable advantage while pointing to actionable priorities—targeting high-value AI use cases, conducting capability audits, institutionalizing continuous learning through visible leadership and role-based upskilling, and embedding culture- and skills-oriented KPIs within AI governance. © 2025 by the authors.},
