	abstract = {Large language models (LLMs) are rapidly transforming the banking industry by empowering employees to automate complex tasks, increase productivity, and drive digital transformation across their organizations. However, LLMs also present challenges and limitations related to their stability, accuracy, hallucinations, bias, and increasing regulatory and compliance requirements (e.g., European Union Artificial Intelligence Act). In this article we introduce an LLM governance framework that provides granular, use case-specific, application-level guidelines based on six responsible artificial intelligence pillars for all stages of the LLM product lifecycle. The framework is specifically designed for banks, considering their strong emphasis on risk management and regulatory compliance, and it aims to allow the safe democratization of LLM solutions by ensuring that human oversight, judgment, and accountability remain central throughout the LLM adoption process. By fostering a collaborative environment where human expertise meets LLM capabilities, our framework supports responsible innovation and trust in LLM-driven banking solutions. © 1997-2012 IEEE.},
