	abstract = {We propose a deep learning model pruning method based on the mutual information and the total correlation computation. The solid information theory foundation suggests us the transparent and interpretable pruning strategy for the much-wanted goal of trustworthy AI. We focus on the pruning of the representation layer(s) in an unsupervised manner by measuring the feature redundancy between neurons in the layer(s). The redundancy computation relies on the mutual information then total correlation estimation to measure how two sets of neurons own overlapped information for the pruning to suggest a model with less complexity. Overall, we horizontally focus on the narrowest, assumed to be the most representable layer(s) for the pruning and in the layer(s), find the neuron(s) with the highest redundancy to prune vertically, if having the network models go from the inputs to the outputs horizontally. In short, we propose an interpretable pruning method and one can understand how and why some neurons are pruned from the network. A further issue to address is to have a proper estimation on the mutual information and total correlation. Some theoretical and empirical results confirm the value of the proposed method and the evaluation shows that the proposed pruning method can indeed perform superior or competitively to other state-of-the-art methods even when some of the pruning methods are considered supervised ones. We have tested the proposed method on various network structures such as fully-connected and convolutional networks given real-world datasets for the evaluation. The code for this work is available via https://github.com/NTUST-ML-lab/Interpretable-Deep-Model-Pruning. © 2025},
