	affiliations = {Freeman Spogli Institute for International Studies, Stanford University, Stanford, CA, United States},
	abstract = {This paper examines how the 2025 U.S. ban on TikTok triggered an unexpected policy backfire when millions of American users migrated to RedNote (Xiaohongshu), another Chinese-owned platform. Following the Supreme Court's decision in TikTok Inc. v. Garland, over 4 million U.S. users - primarily digital opinion leaders - deliberately chose RedNote as an act of political protest, creating unprecedented cross-cultural connections that amplified rather than reduced Chinese digital influence. This analysis is based on over 300 pages of congressional testimony, the full transcript of oral arguments from the Supreme Court's TikTok Inc. v. Garland decision, and third-party platform analytics from sources such as SimilarWeb and SensorTower. In addition, the paper draws on a targeted review of news coverage and journalist-curated examples of user reactions on TikTok and RedNote during the initial months of the migration. While not a formal content analysis, these sources provide illustrative insight into emergent rhetorical patterns and the broader discursive framing of the migration as protest. Through this, the study identifies five regulatory blind spots that led to this miscalculation: outdated sovereignty frameworks, mischaracterization of user-platform relationships, dismissal of user agency, generational divides in digital literacy, and underappreciation of algorithmic advantages. The RedNote migration reveals fundamental limitations in security-focused governance approaches that fail to recognize platforms as cultural infrastructure central to users' identities, communities, and livelihoods. This case study offers critical insights for policymakers seeking to develop more effective governance frameworks that balance legitimate security concerns with the complex sociotechnical realities of contemporary digital citizenship and presents recommendations for evolving beyond binary ban-permit approaches toward more nuanced regulatory strategies.  © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
