	abstract = {Generative AI is a subset of machine learning focused on generating new, coherent data samples that mimic a given dataset. This chapter introduces core principles and theoretical foundations of generative modeling, emphasizing probabilistic approaches and models such as GANs, VAEs, and transformers like GPT. It explores design strategies, training techniques, and trade-offs involved in quality and efficiency. Practical applications are discussed across domains including natural language generation, synthetic media, drug discovery, medical imaging, education, and content recommendation. Real-world case studies support the theoretical insights. The chapter also addresses ethical considerations like deepfakes, data privacy, and algorithmic fairness, promoting responsible AI development. Emerging trends such as diffusion models and multimodal generation are also covered, offering a concise yet comprehensive guide for researchers and practitioners in academic, industrial, and cross-disciplinary settings. © 2026, IGI Global Scientific Publishing. All rights reserved.},
