	abstract = {The rise of artificial intelligence (AI) has significantly impacted the creative domain, once considered exclusive to humans, sparking intense academic debate regarding the authenticity of AI creativity and the protection of AI-generated works. This paper critically examines dominant technological culture ideologies across various posthuman societal scenarios and employs the “4P Perspectives of Machine Creativity” framework to assess the legitimacy of AI creativity. It analyzes the dilemmas and anthropocentric constraints embedded in current legal approaches and proposes context-specific protection strategies for AI-generated works. The study finds that the underlying cultural ideologies shaping Industry 4.0+5.0, Cyborg Society, and Technological Singularity scenarios have influenced the evolution of AI ethics and legal interpretations. While the Person, Process, and Product dimensions confirm the existence of AI creativity, legal and cultural norms under the Press dimension remain the main barriers to its recognition. The reluctance to legally protect AI-generated works reflects persistent anthropocentric biases, which may ultimately undermine the interests of human creators. This paper recommends allocating rights within the existing copyright framework based on AI's “distributed agency” in the Industry 4.0+5.0 scenario, and exploring limited or full legal personhood for AI in more advanced posthuman contexts to ensure appropriate rights attribution. © 2025 The Authors},
	author_keywords = {AI; Anthropocentrism; Creativity; Legal protection; Posthumanism},
