	abstract = {This study develops an interpretable machine learning model for thickness uniformity control in 20-high rolling mill of 316L stainless steel foil. Innovatively incorporating work hardening rate derived from differential Voce constitutive modeling, the proposed method bridges materials plasticity theory with data-driven prediction. A SHAP-based interpretability mechanism reveals the coupled effects of work hardening behavior and rolling parameters on thickness deviation, overcoming the black-box limitation of conventional prediction models. The proposed BO-LSTM framework, incorporating n-value dynamics and rolling parameters, outperforms conventional models with MAE = 0.000101 mm, RMSE = 0.000137 mm, and R² = 0.9372. Actual production demonstrates significant improvements in thickness consistency compared to empirical process settings, while the explainable AI framework provides physical insights into the ultra-thin foil rolling mechanism. The methodology establishes a new paradigm for intelligent control of high-precision cold rolling systems through physics-informed machine learning. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd},
