	title = {Revised Strengthening the Reporting of Cohort, Cross-Sectional and Case-Control Studies in Surgery (STROCSS) Guideline: An Update for the Age of Artificial Intelligence},
	year = {2025},
	journal = {Premier Journal of Science},
	volume = {10},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.70389/PJS.100081},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105012265370?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Premier Science, London, United Kingdom; Royal Free London NHS Foundation Trust, London, United Kingdom; Imperial College School of Medicine, London, United Kingdom; Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health, Boston, MA, United States; University College London Hospital, London, United Kingdom; Wellington Regional Hospital, Te Whatu Ora Capital Coast and Hutt Valley, Wellington, New Zealand; Imperial College London, London, United Kingdom; McMaster University, Canada; Sapienza University of Rome, Italy; Swansea Bay University Health Board, Swansea University, United Kingdom; Almana Hospital, Khobar, Saudi Arabia; Manipal Academy of Higher Education Manipal, India; MIOT Hospital, Chennai, India; Guy’s and St Thomas’ Hospitals, United Kingdom; Tata Memorial Hospital, Homi Bhabha National Institute and National Cancer Grid, India; IRCCS-ISMETT – UPMC Italy, Italy; Harvard Medical School, United States; Cactus Communications Pvt Ltd, India; Saglik Bilimleri Universitesi, Turkey; NHS Lothian, United Kingdom; Staten Island University Hospital Northwell Health, United States; Queen Elizabeth University Hospital Glasgow and Institute for Cancer Therapeutics University of Bradford, United Kingdom; Changi General Hospital, Singapore; Cook County Health, United States; Hospital Clinico San Carlos, Madrid, Spain; District Hospital Udupi, India; Fatimah Hospital, Malaysia; Aga Khan University, Pakistan; Centre for Cancer Screening, Prevention and Early Diagnosis, Wolfson Institute of Population Health, Queen Mary University of London, London, United Kingdom; Breast Services, Homerton University Hospital, London, United Kingdom; Dell Medical School, UT Austin, United States; Government Medical College, Kashmir, Srinagar, India; Dr. von Hauner Children’s Hospital, LMU Medical Center, Munich, Germany; Nottingham University Hospital, United Kingdom; All India Institute of Medical Sciences, New Delhi, India; P D Hinduja Hospital, Khar, India; Homerton University Hospital, United Kingdom; Tata Memorial Centre, India; Cairo University, Egypt; University of the West Indies Barbados, Barbados; Fondazione Policlinico Campus Biomedico, Italy; Campus Bio Medico University, Italy; UiT The Arctic University of Norway, Norway; Texas A&M University, United States; University of Turku, Finland; Centro di Riferimento Oncologico Aviano IRCCS, Italy; Harefield Hospital, United Kingdom; All India Institute of Medical Sciences Rishikesh, India; Bendigo Health and Monash University, Australia; University College London, United Kingdom; Fondazione Policlinico Campus Bio-Medico di Roma, Italy; Shuguang Hospital, Shanghai University of Traditional Chinese Medicine, China},
	abstract = {INTRODUCTION Artificial intelligence (AI) is rapidly transforming healthcare and scientific publishing. Reporting guidelines need to be updated to take into account this advance. The STROCSS Guideline 2025 update adds a new AI-focused domain to promote transparency, reproducibility, and ethical integrity in surgical observational studies involving AI. METHODS A Delphi consensus exercise was conducted to update the STROCSS guidelines. A panel of 49 surgical and scientific experts were invited to rate proposed new items. In Round 1, participants scored each item on a nine-point Likert scale and provided feedback. Items not meeting consensus were revised or discarded. RESULTS A 94% response rate occurred among participants (46/49) in the first round. Ratings were analyzed for agreement levels, and consensus was reached on all six proposed AI-related items. A revised STROCSS checklist is presented which incorporates these new AI-related items. Authors are now expected to disclose AI involvement not only in patient care but also in manuscript preparation, as exemplified by this article. CONCLUSION The STROCSS 2025 guideline provides an up-to-date framework for surgical observational studies in the era of AI. Through a robust consensus STROCSS, we have added specific reporting criteria for AI to ensure that any use of artificial intelligence in a surgical observational study is clearly documented, explained, and discussed including with respect to bias and ethics. This update will help maintain the quality, transparency, and clinical relevance of surgical observational studies, ultimately improving their educational value and trustworthiness for the surgical community. © 2025, The Author(s).},
	author_keywords = {AI reporting standards; AI transparency and ethics; Artificial intelligence in surgery; Delphi consensus STROCSS; STROCSS guideline update},
