	affiliations = {Dept. of Mechanical Engineering, York University, Toronto, Canada; Dept. of Computer Engineering, York University, Toronto, Canada},
	abstract = {Engineering students often face delays in getting their questions answered, as professors are occupied with academic and administrative tasks. This leads to gaps in understanding and a lack of immediate support. Students prefer answers linked to professor’s notes rather than excess information, which hinders self-directed learning and the use of visual aids. Core engineering and science courses, or any course deterministic in nature, are well-suited for automated-learning tools that deliver precise answers, unlike open-ended design courses which may have multiple possible answers. This work explores whether an AI-driven tool, based on professors’ notes and course outlines, can improve learning outcomes by providing immediate, relevant answers. Prof-Notes AI is a full-stack application that categorizes queries as technical or syllabus-related and fetches relevant content, including equations, illustrations, and videos. The frontend, developed using React and JavaScript, provides a user-friendly interface, while the Python-based backend integrates AI frameworks like GPT models, Llama, and Pinecone vector databases. It leverages Google App Engine and Firebase for real-time data flow, with caching to optimize performance and reduce computational costs. Security measures like authentication and data encryption protect user data. User testing showed that linking answers directly to professor notes improved response relevance. Many students found Prof-Notes AI reliable, though some noted challenges with complex queries and reliance on other resources. Students appreciated the seamless experience across devices. Future updates will enhance active learning through interactive discussions with the AI. A targeted marketing strategy is needed to raise awareness and integrate the tool into students’ routines, positioning Prof-Notes AI as a key self-directed learning resource for engineering education. © 2025, Canadian Engineering Education Association. All rights reserved.},
