	affiliations = {Oxford Internet Institute, University of Oxford, Oxford, United Kingdom},
	abstract = {This chapter provides a comprehensive introduction to the concept of digital sovereignty, defined broadly as efforts by state and non-state actors to assert authority over digital technologies. It offers a historic overview of how the term has evolved from its early use by 'cyber libertarians' in the 1990s to describe the internet as a space free from state control, to an encompassing concept that describes competition between state and non-state actors to assert authority over a stack of digital technologies. Three case studies are put forward to highlight the different types of competition for digital sovereignty that are taking place. The first centres on the US's efforts to assert greater control over the semiconductor chip supply chain, both domestically and internationally. The second focuses on the conflicts between European governments on the one hand, and Apple and Google on the other, to determine how COVID-19 contact tracing was to be conducted in Europe. The third considers how Apple, Google and Amazon asserted control over the social media platform, Parler, by blocking the company from using their services. Through this theoretical and empirical discussion, the growing importance of digital sovereignty, and the complex dynamics involved in asserting authority over digital technologies are demonstrated. © 2025 John Wiley & Sons Ltd. All rights reserved.},
