	abstract = {This chapter explores AI-driven power flow optimization for sustainable aviation systems, emphasizing its transformative potential in addressing energy efficiency and carbon emission challenges. Aviation systems, characterized by their complex energy demands-including propulsion, avionics, climate control, and ground-based operations-require advanced frameworks for sustainable energy management. Traditional methods often fall short of addressing these dynamic and interconnected demands, leading to inefficiencies. Leveraging artificial intelligence (AI) technologies, particularly machine learning (ML) and reinforcement learning (RL), this study outlines a hybrid AI framework integrating real-time decision-making, predictive analytics, and adaptive energy resource control mechanisms. Methodologies include data preprocessing from diverse aviation datasets, LSTM-based load forecasting, RL for resource allocation, and predictive maintenance using ensemble models. Optimization strategies, such as mixed integer linear programming (MILP) for energy flow and NSGA-II for balancing cost and emissions, are highlighted. System validation employs digital twin technologies, ensuring scalability and resilience under operational complexities. Key performance indicators demonstrate significant enhancements in energy utilization, carbon footprint reduction, and operational cost efficiency. By synergistically integrating renewable energy sources like solar and wind, AI frameworks offer a robust pathway toward greener aviation systems. This chapter concludes by discussing future prospects, including material innovations, system scalability, and interdisciplinary collaborations, to further embed AI-driven methodologies in aviation energy management. The findings contribute a replicable, efficient framework for sustainable energy practices, charting a course for innovation in aviation systems aligned with global sustainability goals. © 2026 Pushan Kumar Dutta, Martin Ricciuti